[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Android IPC: Broadcast Receivers, Content Providers, and Binder"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inter-Process Communication (IPC) is a fundamental pillar of the Android operating system, allowing distinct application processes to securely exchange data and invoke components across process boundaries. In this tutorial, we will explore the core pillars of Android IPC using Kotlin: Broadcast Receivers, Content Providers, and the low-level Binder mechanism."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Broadcast Receivers for Event-Driven IPC"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Broadcast Receivers enable applications to send and receive broadcast messages across the system, resembling the publish-subscribe design pattern. With the introduction of modern Android lifecycle constraints, explicit broadcasting and LocalBroadcastManager have evolved, yet system-wide broadcasts remain vital."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class NetworkStatusReceiver : BroadcastReceiver() {
    override fun onReceive(context: Context, intent: Intent) {
        if (intent.action == "android.net.conn.CONNECTIVITY_CHANGE") {
            // Handle network state change safely
            val isConnected = intent.getBooleanExtra("noConnectivity", false)
            // Process state
        }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Content Providers for Structured Data Sharing"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Content Providers encapsulate data and provide it to applications through a standardized SQLite-like interface. They utilize URIs to address data and implement cursor-based querying, ensuring high-performance data sharing with granular permission control."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class UserContentProvider : ContentProvider() {
    companion object {
        const val AUTHORITY = "com.example.provider.userprovider"
        val CONTENT_URI: Uri = Uri.parse("content://$AUTHORITY/users")
    }

    override fun query(
        uri: Uri,
        projection: Array<String>?,
        selection: String?,
        selectionArgs: Array<String>?,
        sortOrder: String?
    ): Cursor? {
        // Perform secure database query across processes
        return database.query("users", projection, selection, selectionArgs, null, null, sortOrder)
    }

    override fun insert(uri: Uri, values: ContentValues?): Uri? {
        val id = database.insert("users", null, values)
        context?.contentResolver?.notifyChange(uri, null)
        return ContentUris.withAppendedId(CONTENT_URI, id)
    }

    override fun onCreate(): Boolean {
        database = UserDatabaseHelper(context!!).writableDatabase
        return true
    }

    override fun getType(uri: Uri): String = "vnd.android.cursor.dir/vnd.$AUTHORITY.users"
    override fun update(uri: Uri, values: ContentValues?, s: String?, sa: Array<String>?) = 0
    override fun delete(uri: Uri, selection: String?, selectionArgs: Array<String>?) = 0
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Binder Architecture: The Core of Android IPC"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Beneath the high-level components lies Binder, Android\'s lightweight remote procedure call (RPC) mechanism. Binder relies on a kernel driver (/dev/binder) to manage object references and marshal data across process address spaces with a single memory copy operation."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"IBinder Interface: Defines the remote capability protocol."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parcel: Container for message data (State) that is marshaled for transport through Binder."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"AIDL (Android Interface Definition Language): Generates proxy/stub code automatically to simplify Binder usage."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Implementing Custom IPC via AIDL"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To execute custom operations across processes, define an AIDL interface. Android Studio compiles this interface into a Java/Kotlin class containing an abstract Stub class."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// File: IComputationService.aidl
package com.example.ipc;

interface IComputationService {
    int computeSum(int a, int b);
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, implement the generated Stub inside an Android Service exposed to other applications."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"class ComputationService : Service() {
    private val binder = object : IComputationService.Stub() {
        override fun computeSum(a: Int, b: Int): Int {
            // Executes on a Binder thread pool thread, not the main thread
            return a + b
        }
    }

    override fun onBind(intent: Intent): IBinder {
        return binder
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always secure your Content Providers with proper read/write permissions or set exported = false if internal."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep Binder operations thread-safe since AIDL calls are dispatched from a pool of binder threads."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer Content Providers for structured relational data and AIDL/Binder for custom remote procedure calls."}]
